| `doc_drift.forbidden_phrases.<regex>` | string | three phrases true of one machine or one person, and three compound reports of a change (`now` and one of `uses`, `reads`, `accepts`, `rejects`, `supports`, `requires`, `ships`, `runs`; `no longer` and one of `uses`, `accepts`, `rejects`, `supports`, `requires`, or `is no longer used`; `was`, `were`, `has been` or `have been` and one of `changed`, `renamed`, `moved`, then `to` or `from`) | a Python regular expression, mapped to the reason it is refused; the map is read through `scripts/config_loader.py` and must hold at least one entry | `tests/integration/test_doc_drift.py`; `tests/integration/test_copier_generation.py` | Shipped prose that matches a listed expression fails the test with that reason; a hard-wrapped sentence is read with its line breaks joined, and the finding names the line the match starts on. A verb the corpus uses in a present claim stays out of the report expressions: `no longer reads` states a present limit in this file and matches none of the three, and `test_a_real_present_claim_in_the_corpus_stays_accepted` keeps that claim green. A generated project's own copy of the test gives the same verdict. The sibling collection is `change_comments.phrases`: it fails a code comment that opens with an entry, and in prose it judges only a thought's opener, as advice. | a literal host, port or path: refuse the phrase that introduces it | always |
